<p>{{ site.name }} is owned and operated by {{ site.operator }}, {{ site.entityDescription }}. A factual error in a published article should be corrected on the article, not quietly deleted. This page is our corrections policy. It is not legal advice.</p>
<h2>What we correct</h2>
<p>We correct a fact that is wrong: a name, number, date, quote, or description of a document that does not match the source. We also correct a headline or summary that says something the article’s sources do not support.</p>
<p>We do not change an article to match a preference, a missing compliment, or a disagreement about emphasis when the cited fact is accurate. Opinion, analysis, and speculation stay labeled. A labeled opinion is not a factual error by itself.</p>
<h2>How to ask</h2>
<p>Write to <a href="{{ '/contact/' | url }}">the desk</a>. Include the article URL, the passage, and a public source a reader can open. Say who you are if you want a reply. We do not publish a street address.</p>
<h2>What a correction looks like</h2>
<p>The article keeps a note of what was wrong and what it says now. The visible <strong>Last updated</strong> date changes, and the page’s structured data <code>dateModified</code> uses that same <code>updatedAt</code> value. The original publication date stays in place.</p>
<p>If the whole article is unsound, we say so on the page instead of pretending it was never published. When a passage is in doubt and we cannot check it, we take it out.</p>
<p>The standards behind that choice are on <a href="{{ '/sources-ethics/' | url }}">Sources &amp; Ethics</a>.</p>
